#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171683 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172105 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21ac91dd-ea0e-5432-8431-f5fa4d32c6bb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals Given Two Normal Populations"}] :question/id #uuid "7d75f249-230d-5252-9db1-31f142ed7e70" :question/math-academy-id "e-10062" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n_1=29$ from a normal population with standard deviation $\\sigma_1=7$ and a sample of size $n_2=23$ from a normal population with standard deviation $\\sigma_2=9.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are $\\overline{x}_1=15$ and  $\\overline{x}_2=9,$ find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the difference $\\mu_1-\\mu_2$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z > 1.96) = 0.025,$ where $Z \\sim N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*\n\n![](images/69/693576483310a4656435ea4a3d8d9652b8de563d26a87c7c80fb902a3bef1e29.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the difference $\\mu_1-\\mu_2$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n(\\overline{x}_1-\\overline{x}_2) \\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E=z_{\\alpha/2} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_1^2}{n_1}+\\dfrac{\\sigma_2^2}{n_2}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- $\\overline{x}_1$ and $\\overline{x}_2$ are the sample means, $\\mu_1$ and $\\mu_2$ are the population means, $\\sigma_1^2$ and $\\sigma_2^2$ are the population variances,  $n_1$ and $n_2$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}_1-\\overline{x}_2 = 15-9 = 6\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z > 1.96) = 0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.025} = 1.96.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE = 1.96 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{7^2}{29}+\\dfrac{9^2}{23}} \\approx 4.474\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 6\\pm 4.474\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171684 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172106 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21507a23-f729-56aa-8e9c-66eb5823a2d8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals Given Two Large Samples"}] :question/id #uuid "b8eff294-354a-5622-acfe-ca148de26225" :question/math-academy-id "e-10063" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n_1=68$ from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma_1=6$ and a sample of size $n_2=35$ from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma_2=5.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are $\\overline{x}_1=18$ and  $\\overline{x}_2=15,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the difference $\\mu_1-\\mu_2$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z > 1.645) = 0.05,$ where $Z \\sim N(0,1).$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\geq30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the difference $\\mu_1-\\mu_2$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n(\\overline{x}_1-\\overline{x}_2) \\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E=z_{\\alpha/2} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_1^2}{n_1}+\\dfrac{\\sigma_2^2}{n_2}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- $\\overline{x}_1$ and $\\overline{x}_2$ are the sample means, $\\mu_1$ and $\\mu_2$ are the population means, $\\sigma_1^2$ and $\\sigma_2^2$ are the population variances,  $n_1$ and $n_2$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}_1-\\overline{x}_2 = 18-15 = 3\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z > 1.645) = 0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.05} = 1.645.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE = 1.645 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{6^2}{68}+\\dfrac{5^2}{35}} \\approx 1.835\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $90\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 3\\pm 1.835\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171709 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172129 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "be6d2ce2-f7cc-5a87-86d1-21f769dbd580" :knowledge-point/title "Applications of Confidence Intervals"}] :question/id #uuid "ce645810-49c8-5b0c-8fb4-934f3b65f900" :question/math-academy-id "e-11111" :question/problem "The diameters of balls for bearings produced by machines ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) and ![](images/ab/ab4817775494786b06f0b9cecf225ea5979c035b69c36c1fa078f835524be9e9.svg) have standard deviations of ![](images/d1/d119f2a860c3a45855fddc0be7b90afe3ef3b9c9a1d9e12f6f292887360e797d.svg) and ![](images/47/47d7f608a187818bc771a90371ec6ed6fd92a8446815fb911253e4f545627c81.svg) respectively. Engineers want to know whether the population mean ![](images/61/61959f998d5bf057f78b23b849c800b479b5640bee3f604626eb721872cffc20.svg) for the diameter of balls produced by machine ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) is larger than the population mean ![](images/55/550e73c341b931075845607f5f4bcece014f3088cfb1148197b696933d13545a.svg) for the diameter of balls produced by machine ![](images/02/029615e92d8b6d5c444a4daf74a9a8d63048e8d4be5f97c8207fe7af2e189e18.svg) They construct a ![](images/4a/4aa35f22590ee68ad0ff0b07b01c41e9caf9e2ad6c73a305f297e918d684e045.svg) confidence interval for the difference between the population means.   \n\nGiven that the engineers sampled ![](images/8c/8c0d9d65f3495da5c81dd0e397e9c32f09f2440810847b109055a8cdf9b63c1d.svg) balls produced by machine ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) and ![](images/45/4554df746f5a593617a7882989a71995d6e7a6be8ef47225843dafb9e8d746e3.svg) balls made by machine ![](images/ab/ab4817775494786b06f0b9cecf225ea5979c035b69c36c1fa078f835524be9e9.svg) and found that their mean diameters are ![](images/3a/3ac1972c8c6e03b02a5569c5ccf4f0cd79744fc783050367109d053c72c56e2e.svg) and ![](images/b3/b3294c6196c0bb447f4261b566f3f910eff97c9591565bc2b35ac7798c3a5e97.svg) respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The end-points of the ![](images/4a/4aa35f22590ee68ad0ff0b07b01c41e9caf9e2ad6c73a305f297e918d684e045.svg) confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately ![](images/be/be02ea5f949b002400b734a0b683d09930c91c625e8be7d7a44a4eae822476c9.svg)\n\nII. All values contained within the ![](images/4a/4aa35f22590ee68ad0ff0b07b01c41e9caf9e2ad6c73a305f297e918d684e045.svg) confidence interval are positive.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ![](images/00/00d6a9d8ff1bdcb7095099ba2d0b2192b4df6bf4328ee093825ec36db7191df5.svg)\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable ![](images/bf/bf4994a6e13924c5bc1f82242e6b12a6e452ebdb5c9f607b35c2614dab4ba30e.svg) the table below shows the ![](images/90/9032cb23a878659e74b670d3779a8cd4793b28af921eab1a6b3f2041a815e03b.svg)-scores ![](images/f9/f948084d308bf70eed90f1ddc186f4d4b6ffd5b03b4a84db77741ca7acb0697c.svg) such that ![](images/2e/2e84a088a2421fdf8a026b804a2a39d41831915f78d6ca5abcf7c95644ea3f98.svg) for some particular values of ![](images/1d/1d3e45623fb292952d8c207690797505cc7c484a6410f4a95aff94a51b4a0ee5.svg)*\n\n| ![](images/48/48c7b594e8628a17b991d4e65e7bee6ffe6680dd410f036cfae83154bbd55fc6.svg) | ![](images/14/14eb5f107102d64032e5c62207a5f22c7080993cccdec49809d85677e32228dc.svg) | ![](images/f4/f40b54dcb945a64594b78fd581e4f950c4d9c07d15f30d9f1dc019f46c02cdde.svg) | ![](images/11/1156c0c032864aee44a0c788d626a41dc2f342323fa089a19d82027b0ae5a2e4.svg) | ![](images/96/96bea0a68c23edf8d74b7757824baa031b2b7d0bbabd8a9babe5b9bf72a0a13e.svg) | ![](images/bf/bf7be53403f336e34ad868c68d24905fb2eafba87ae7c65ebfa0796fd137efe0.svg) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ![](images/f9/f948084d308bf70eed90f1ddc186f4d4b6ffd5b03b4a84db77741ca7acb0697c.svg) | ![](images/12/12781236356b8a089115083f90b012544e848846f64395d4b9e0a5f2ca689c93.svg) | ![](images/b5/b5f9496f7a1bda32cf8f921e43a5bb42c820b118ebe241131cd7a38b869061b7.svg) | ![](images/0e/0e11b9a3e1e1a424a8db948ce46afebd8abc9faa855574ea12b8760c1da2ebf9.svg) | ![](images/18/185c18f81476cc288ddc92a0105a258c4b0e89893c75c7df3a6b366d6cdcf9ee.svg) | ![](images/13/1345290aa983a990a0e9592b1156be4bd26490599086e86ec7ccf83313f1412e.svg) |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., ![](images/7e/7e953b8222eba132ef65fbeead25790198f988c2e0cd5625aa5de0fda0fefa1a.svg) and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value ![](images/79/7995b2daef1650484d2fa6518cb775356ae12ecdf597eba9e2e04f4f35dee93c.svg) between ![](images/15/156b31ede73de5301befe67f02745a0202f1806a62f9fdecc6fc9ee3f21117ba.svg) and ![](images/76/76543f4805f22715457d55feb2a3c87d0d873bdcb9c1eed7a4f68539269dcf59.svg) the corresponding ![](images/1f/1fb6279301c8fe6dce4a6f502a17e6e4abeaa62a44a9ab5023a1d3ccf4aac5c4.svg) confidence interval for the difference ![](images/9d/9d6dac96cd69f7064940eae6e63d88c95f1fc4a92b102ba3693dd819ac128126.svg) between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n![](images/5d/5d808254159d48a94531e110e5d9c4c8fabc2f800c5615802abccfa7bc8ea622.svg)\n\nwhere \n\n- ![](images/4b/4b7b988b2e9ef88f674ce655d7d2f15af2f4e9172bd7a9d6cce2fe0aa72e82df.svg) is the margin of error,\n\n- ![](images/69/6937033abbb07640e7bfa8def543b2a045bb5e1775a939ab76a5fcca1cb0a0ea.svg) and ![](images/fc/fca756bcf0457e144eb84a5bfd67bb3f4432d22de2e7d0f3c5fb2e9c78727da9.svg) are the sample means, ![](images/6d/6d3486d14fef4cbd7f15af9097617bd5e675baa1f13cb86f69f09bef24ee4829.svg) and ![](images/a2/a21f07d3948532bb6aa91995da9b6d602ad4b8628d9ea967ad3f3d135f9ba533.svg) are the population means, ![](images/20/20f6be5e75cac9315a72adf7baab3c361dbb69fe57709526e03c9a023af587af.svg) and ![](images/65/65622d587fccd0fe592e6a5dce2ed965999dd2e90a288087469f8c4c70eb3503.svg) are the population variances,  ![](images/de/de44911177478b672559d6311b30df7c906693cd480fe64d8dcf78c08412d61e.svg) and ![](images/07/072df845480f1dd134dc71854a3d9aeacde7563aaca739d5c718f5068d5ee300.svg) are the sample sizes,\n\n- ![](images/af/af38b9349332c35a9833820ab5fa0f3540b7655405c8b2ca17587f3db84f53bf.svg) and ![](images/c1/c1a5b6740351318fb48420ebbed2f09086cd253fb88b802c8f6bf138640d75fa.svg)\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n![](images/c1/c1d47fdc3028b6d69a77ad99d91ad6031d852da3aad276e0ff8e792b12edecc3.svg)\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a ![](images/4a/4aa35f22590ee68ad0ff0b07b01c41e9caf9e2ad6c73a305f297e918d684e045.svg) confidence interval, we have \n![](images/bb/bb227d4b2458830d95e344377c1633020fb3ce3bc3f27acfb7ec28f3473c6548.svg)\nWe also need to find the ![](images/90/9032cb23a878659e74b670d3779a8cd4793b28af921eab1a6b3f2041a815e03b.svg)-score value ![](images/ff/ff6062321d513483e6075fddac7ccaa5d848044aa39968f15e2e120e23e09cc4.svg) such that ![](images/98/98124d1c0a053f7a1651a849ef0f233f42ca9c8bae4154aaed3bd995b88dcdb7.svg) where ![](images/c1/c1a5b6740351318fb48420ebbed2f09086cd253fb88b802c8f6bf138640d75fa.svg)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ![](images/5b/5b048df55a97617e12801c6e121edf26dfc90b10795b7fb3c3e57990511bc3ca.svg) \n\n| ![](images/48/48c7b594e8628a17b991d4e65e7bee6ffe6680dd410f036cfae83154bbd55fc6.svg) | ![](images/14/14eb5f107102d64032e5c62207a5f22c7080993cccdec49809d85677e32228dc.svg) | ![](images/f4/f40b54dcb945a64594b78fd581e4f950c4d9c07d15f30d9f1dc019f46c02cdde.svg) | ![](images/11/1156c0c032864aee44a0c788d626a41dc2f342323fa089a19d82027b0ae5a2e4.svg) | ![](images/96/96bea0a68c23edf8d74b7757824baa031b2b7d0bbabd8a9babe5b9bf72a0a13e.svg) | ![](images/c9/c9b3bdfe48f79c3efdc3f67699556323b7f2df004dc4e9957e2e2fd18a98494c.svg) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ![](images/f9/f948084d308bf70eed90f1ddc186f4d4b6ffd5b03b4a84db77741ca7acb0697c.svg) | ![](images/12/12781236356b8a089115083f90b012544e848846f64395d4b9e0a5f2ca689c93.svg) | ![](images/b5/b5f9496f7a1bda32cf8f921e43a5bb42c820b118ebe241131cd7a38b869061b7.svg) | ![](images/0e/0e11b9a3e1e1a424a8db948ce46afebd8abc9faa855574ea12b8760c1da2ebf9.svg) | ![](images/18/185c18f81476cc288ddc92a0105a258c4b0e89893c75c7df3a6b366d6cdcf9ee.svg) | ![](images/15/15b83a8ca53afabe815ab94d820effad249c1e7b05bb06c20bf94badc051a9da.svg) |\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n![](images/4a/4a3f5d764d317efe5be018c3be111afdb40da1316dc74fe9ccf8037274260ee7.svg) \n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n![](images/0c/0c73cf36767ddad8a08ec9fe256a146af7d251921180cdb7397c69a5a2fa6baf.svg)\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The end-points of our confidence interval are ![](images/be/be02ea5f949b002400b734a0b683d09930c91c625e8be7d7a44a4eae822476c9.svg)\n\n- Statement II is false. The confidence interval contains zero.\n![](images/a0/a08641be702b83463c7c420e7ae8f77d69564f4c1734f3d809efe7a4027fed7e.svg)\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the confidence interval contains zero, it remains possible that ![](images/52/52ee5d6a0009f029e4a9917fbff9deecf964de59a9bea34c3461695582c597ba.svg)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172369 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172129 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "be6d2ce2-f7cc-5a87-86d1-21f769dbd580" :knowledge-point/title "Applications of Confidence Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2465302-e38b-5022-97f1-e3508fe38200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169078} {:answer/id #uuid "e45a9763-7472-5249-8d87-0217a4f64059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169079} {:answer/id #uuid "655be236-db0f-5619-97b4-155d41b0cc52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169080} {:answer/id #uuid "66f7c51b-3ded-581d-bd3f-c423369d6bfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169081} {:answer/id #uuid "7d26245b-397f-5b69-8629-5b522cf34b7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d26245b-397f-5b69-8629-5b522cf34b7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169082} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c93f752-af6d-5f66-b0e2-647fec3249d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e988df3e-2b97-5c9e-988c-9ed264a72932" :question/math-academy-id "q-159507" :question/problem "The scores on the final calculus test in states X and Y are known to be normally distributed with standard deviations of ${\\sigma }_{x}=5$ points and ${\\sigma }_{y}=4$ points, respectively. Scientists want to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ of scores in state X is smaller than the population mean of scores in state Y. They decide to construct a $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.\n\nThe scientists sampled $35$ students from state X and $23$ students from state Y, finding that their mean scores are $85$ points and $87$ points, respectively. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. The end-points of the $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $-2\\pm 2.105.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $90%$ confidence interval are negative.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}<{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=85-87=-2\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.05}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.05})=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.05}=1.645:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{5}^{2}}{35}+\\frac{{4}^{2}}{23}}≈1.953\n$$\n\n \n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -2\\pm 1.953\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The end-points of our confidence interval are $-2\\pm 1.953.$\n\n- Statement II is true. All values within the confidence interval are negative:\n\n$$\n(-2-1.953,\\,-2+1.953)=(-3.953,\\,-0.047)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since all values within the confidence interval are negative, this suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}<{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172129 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "be6d2ce2-f7cc-5a87-86d1-21f769dbd580" :knowledge-point/title "Applications of Confidence Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa81f763-d71e-55a8-bbff-21322b0f02ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169084} {:answer/id #uuid "c5edff8e-8184-5105-b9b7-17b0972a2a02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169085} {:answer/id #uuid "edca4d6e-454a-5121-975b-a532593b0ff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169086} {:answer/id #uuid "210ba582-23de-501e-b9c9-ec249b9eaa85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169087} {:answer/id #uuid "5509fb24-0bc7-591c-ad40-49e542610ab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5edff8e-8184-5105-b9b7-17b0972a2a02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169085} :answer-field/id #uuid "98fc0260-b7b2-5eca-b4e2-0bac2e279c25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ad1ddf45-bbd7-5a1b-a376-1cbf70f7de9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-159508" :question/problem "Previous studies show that the scores of girls and boys on a particular test for dyslexia have standard deviations of $2$ points and $4$ points, respectively. Scientists want to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ for girls is the same as the population mean ${\\mu }_{y}$ for boys. They construct a $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.\n\nScientists sampled $125$ girls and $118$ boys and found that their mean scores on the test were $42$ points and $41$ points. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The end-points of the $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $1\\pm 1.055.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $99%$ confidence interval are positive.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}>{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=42-41=1\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.05}=2.576:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{2}^{2}}{125}+\\frac{{4}^{2}}{118}}≈1.055\n$$\n\n \n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 1\\pm 1.055\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The end-points of our confidence interval are $1\\pm 1.055.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The confidence interval contains $0:$\n\n$$\n(1-1.055,\\,1+1.055)=(-0.055,\\,2.055)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the confidence interval contains zero, it remains possible that ${\\mu }_{x}={\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500344 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172129 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "be6d2ce2-f7cc-5a87-86d1-21f769dbd580" :knowledge-point/title "Applications of Confidence Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19465a71-2c42-5c5b-bf95-b79015398ab2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499707} {:answer/id #uuid "1d59393f-1c83-55c1-a14b-2582c48643bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499718} {:answer/id #uuid "201cc3b4-0358-5baa-8b26-c3707f290a81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499726} {:answer/id #uuid "469786b7-4252-564e-a019-cd79a0addf4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499786} {:answer/id #uuid "5fb0b782-7d29-5719-901e-f1c7ad53889c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "469786b7-4252-564e-a019-cd79a0addf4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499786} :answer-field/id #uuid "8efbc2d7-abce-5db8-9ce0-ff02e6978bfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500236}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b65f05d5-c649-50dc-a60f-542de8b1eeb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-159625" :question/problem "The weights of two age groups of men, X and Y, are normally distributed with a standard deviation of  ${\\sigma }_{x}=10\\,\\text{kg},$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}=12\\,\\text{kg},$ respectively.\nA researcher wants to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ of the men's weights in group X is less than the population mean ${\\mu }_{y}$ of the men's weights in group Y. He decides to construct a $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.\n\nThe researcher sampled $28$ men from group X and $30$ men from group Y, finding that their mean weights are $74\\,kg$ and $82\\,kg,$ respectively. Which of the following statements are true?  \n\nI. The end-points of the $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $-8\\pm 4.005.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $99%$ confidence interval are negative.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}<{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=74-82=-8\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005}=2.576:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{10}^{2}}{28}+\\frac{{12}^{2}}{30}}≈7.453\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -8\\pm 7.453\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The end-points of our confidence interval are $-8\\pm 7.453.$\n\n- Statement II is true. All values within the confidence interval are negative:\n\n$$\n(-8-7.453,\\,-8+7.453)=(-15.453,\\,-0.547)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since all values within the confidence interval are negative, this suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}<{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500352 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172106 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21507a23-f729-56aa-8e9c-66eb5823a2d8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals Given Two Large Samples"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "107fe77d-b432-5e5d-a714-2ad39721738a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14\\pm 1.96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499691} {:answer/id #uuid "1099919c-3ad8-52a0-bf85-4786225bb35b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14\\pm 0.981" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499692} {:answer/id #uuid "5977604f-0c53-576d-9bf5-ffb432edcde9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14\\pm 1.922" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499826} {:answer/id #uuid "a382ec2a-1be0-55d9-8223-ffcaf3323116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14\\pm 0.719" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499975} {:answer/id #uuid "e1ccb036-87c7-57c1-b7c2-4bc6a0ecf5eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14\\pm 1.387" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1ccb036-87c7-57c1-b7c2-4bc6a0ecf5eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14\\pm 1.387" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500128} :answer-field/id #uuid "036316b9-78c6-568f-97e6-f24937589101" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2323ee0-90b5-5053-934c-27a812c3f1ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-159627" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=46$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=4$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=67$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=3.2.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=17$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=31,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=17-31=-14\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{4}^{2}}{46}+\\frac{{3.2}^{2}}{67}}≈1.387\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -14\\pm 1.387\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500316 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172106 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21507a23-f729-56aa-8e9c-66eb5823a2d8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals Given Two Large Samples"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5659876a-67ff-5346-a481-846ce012b452" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pm 1.256" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499821} {:answer/id #uuid "79750ee5-faa4-5ac2-81c0-5577db5d5bd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pm 0.560" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499881} {:answer/id #uuid "926dfafc-ce4a-5046-951b-9ccf381dc6bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pm 0.533" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499939} {:answer/id #uuid "a9bcad9a-b7e1-5665-9bbe-0e18f5490c65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pm 1.232" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499987} {:answer/id #uuid "c6928ebd-d517-53d2-9d99-868dd521cf49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pm 0.937" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6928ebd-d517-53d2-9d99-868dd521cf49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pm 0.937" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500055} :answer-field/id #uuid "13f570ad-c43b-57e8-ba93-0fc1150a2f90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500190}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a88f00c-6713-5649-aec8-434c7227c5ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-159629" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=62$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=2$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=37$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=3.1.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=5$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=2,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=5-2=3\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{2}^{2}}{62}+\\frac{{3.1}^{2}}{37}}≈0.937\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 3\\pm 0.937\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500359 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172106 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21507a23-f729-56aa-8e9c-66eb5823a2d8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals Given Two Large Samples"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f0f6794-b4ff-5127-a887-5ccf8da54017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\pm 1.517" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499689} {:answer/id #uuid "1ef338df-0f9e-5b97-8308-552bcb3b86cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\pm 1.833" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499722} {:answer/id #uuid "229c5f18-9463-50d8-822d-ccc6b223d2b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\pm 1.038" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499731} {:answer/id #uuid "bd94bfad-cadb-5ab7-a2fb-2585eb0f5a6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\pm 1.655" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500041} {:answer/id #u