of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=53-52=1\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{7.2}^{2}}{128}+\\frac{{4}^{2}}{49}}≈1.676\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 1\\pm 1.676\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172373 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172106 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21507a23-f729-56aa-8e9c-66eb5823a2d8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals Given Two Large Samples"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "085068f5-8a74-5a8c-ab35-10473d7d13ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7\\pm 2.461" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499670} {:answer/id #uuid "1d63990b-f2f6-55fc-b457-f831826e44c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7\\pm 2.518" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499719} {:answer/id #uuid "6a0db282-85d1-5448-ab0f-a0a104792929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7\\pm 1.013" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499857} {:answer/id #uuid "7bb68cf4-10fe-55ad-84c2-8e802a1021ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7\\pm 3.185" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499885} {:answer/id #uuid "92d0f8f0-eb37-55f9-9982-76818ef21ecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7\\pm 2.576" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d63990b-f2f6-55fc-b457-f831826e44c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7\\pm 2.518" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499719} :answer-field/id #uuid "d16abb27-7c82-5554-8c75-0525b40fdf06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d2bd4cad-a5c6-5dae-8046-6824cc91a2bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-159638" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=57$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=7$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=94$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=3.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=22$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=29,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=22-29=-7\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{7}^{2}}{57}+\\frac{{3}^{2}}{94}}≈2.518\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -7\\pm 2.518\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500301 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172129 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "be6d2ce2-f7cc-5a87-86d1-21f769dbd580" :knowledge-point/title "Applications of Confidence Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e8bfbf9-a916-5b30-9995-bcb268797646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499687} {:answer/id #uuid "1eff722a-3a3d-57c9-96db-e3afde6bd3f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499724} {:answer/id #uuid "dad6bc31-1c34-52a6-9bef-8f2fa704739b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500104} {:answer/id #uuid "f01f181e-82f2-5865-9bc5-a4b20ee3a50d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500150} {:answer/id #uuid "fbe0d9ad-5199-54ff-8dac-b93c7a287ead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e8bfbf9-a916-5b30-9995-bcb268797646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499687} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fb8f9ee-2d32-5724-b54f-4283d651b51b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "394c3561-19e2-5323-928c-73d8d3b37d89" :question/math-academy-id "q-159639" :question/problem "It is known that the weights of oranges from farms X and Y are normally distributed with standard deviations of ${\\sigma }_{x}=7$ grams and ${\\sigma }_{y}=8$ grams, respectively. The owner of the farms wants to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ of the weights of oranges from farm X is smaller than the population mean ${\\mu }_{y}$ of the weights of oranges from farm Y. He decides to construct a $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.\n\nThe farmer sampled $25$ oranges from farm X and $20$ oranges from farm Y, finding that their mean weights are $125$ grams and $128.2$ grams, respectively. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The end-points of the $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $-3.2\\pm 3.737.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $90%$ confidence interval are negative.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}<{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=125-128.2=-3.2\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.05}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.05})=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.05}=1.645:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{7}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{8}^{2}}{20}}≈3.737\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -3.2\\pm 3.737\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The end-points of our confidence interval are $-3.2\\pm 3.737.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The confidence interval contains $0:$ \n\n$$\n(-3.2-3.737,\\,-3.2+3.737)=(-6.937,\\,0.537)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the confidence interval contains zero, it remains possible that ${\\mu }_{x}={\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500368 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172129 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "be6d2ce2-f7cc-5a87-86d1-21f769dbd580" :knowledge-point/title "Applications of Confidence Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a8bbfc0-e7fe-52a8-ae44-1d26fdf713f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499710} {:answer/id #uuid "2aeb8b8c-08de-50a8-a2b4-a4dac8256ee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499747} {:answer/id #uuid "7d81a17f-36f8-5f95-9e05-bf828e988da1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499890} {:answer/id #uuid "801293a6-a6ee-5582-abdd-6c2eac5cec4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499896} {:answer/id #uuid "dc5f7146-ae17-5c2f-89c8-94d3090bce16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc5f7146-ae17-5c2f-89c8-94d3090bce16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500107} :answer-field/id #uuid "ede57a7a-8e37-5d0b-866a-4f7edf2e1203" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0803d61-1e04-581c-a011-ddebaf5fd353" :question/math-academy-id "q-159644" :question/problem "It is known that sales (quantities) of a new product in two different states X and Y are normally distributed with standard deviations of ${\\sigma }_{x}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}=7$, respectively. A marketing agency wants to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ of sales in state X is greater than the population mean ${\\mu }_{y}$ of sales in state Y. They decide to construct a $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the two population means.\n\nThe agency sampled $14$ shops from state X and $15$ shops from state Y, finding that their mean sales were $54.5$ and $48.6$, respectively. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The end-points of the $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $5.9\\pm 7.630.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $99%$ confidence interval are positive.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}>{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=54.5-48.6=5.9\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.05}=2.576:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\n \n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{5}^{2}}{14}+\\frac{{7}^{2}}{15}}≈5.790\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 5.9\\pm 5.790\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The end-points of our confidence interval are $5.9\\pm 5.790.$\n\n- Statement II is true. All values within the confidence interval are positive:\n\n$$\n(5.9-5.790,\\,5.9+5.790)=(0.11,\\,11.69)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since all values within the confidence interval are positive, this suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}>{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172105 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21ac91dd-ea0e-5432-8431-f5fa4d32c6bb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals Given Two Normal Populations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4973b038-5f39-5534-aee7-34713e78281b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pm 3.184" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499792} {:answer/id #uuid "4b82ae34-fc82-5522-95ce-288cdfc60c14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pm 2.617" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499799} {:answer/id #uuid "9813fb09-9a2d-5dcc-a465-e9389bfcf2bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pm 3.109" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499950} {:answer/id #uuid "bf1bcfde-923f-5031-a349-fb1978beefc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pm 2.911" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500044} {:answer/id #uuid "e1e7cc53-3921-5765-9657-d7965a1dd19c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pm 3.456" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf1bcfde-923f-5031-a349-fb1978beefc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pm 2.911" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500044} :answer-field/id #uuid "da96cd01-655c-5aad-b1ac-38d2723dadfa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500269}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "157efc5d-8a57-58a8-ada2-5fc55f4e29c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-159646" :question/problem "![](images/69/693576483310a4656435ea4a3d8d9652b8de563d26a87c7c80fb902a3bef1e29.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=14$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=3$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=16$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=5.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=9$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=7,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=9-7=2\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{3}^{2}}{14}+\\frac{{5}^{2}}{16}}≈2.911\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 2\\pm 2.911\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172105 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21ac91dd-ea0e-5432-8431-f5fa4d32c6bb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals Given Two Normal Populations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "283a3441-c0c0-5d33-b1e2-95d020430232" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\pm 2.687" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499741} {:answer/id #uuid "9dbb7478-fadc-55a0-8cdc-5d1784873613" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\pm 2.619" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499960} {:answer/id #uuid "c01c198b-0134-5374-bf17-62d16cb81133" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\pm 2.879" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500046} {:answer/id #uuid "d9e07a6a-8240-5544-96c1-be08f41d01a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\pm 2.771" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500100} {:answer/id #uuid "dce3af0e-2ba7-5bdc-aa32-0401e94fd7d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\pm 2.817" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500108}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9e07a6a-8240-5544-96c1-be08f41d01a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\pm 2.771" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500100} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2318c08-626c-5f9c-998d-a8b0cc21f17c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "31355969-2364-544d-b379-930b8824ee1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-159648" :question/problem "![](images/39/39a83b1081d22919c9549c4ff1c6ad8006dc802cc23f9802d762e97883bea48c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=23$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=28$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=7.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=21$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=25,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=21-25=-4\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{5}^{2}}{23}+\\frac{{7}^{2}}{28}}≈2.771\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -4\\pm 2.771\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500314 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172105 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21ac91dd-ea0e-5432-8431-f5fa4d32c6bb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals Given Two Normal Populations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47fed120-1d44-5ea5-a77d-e3c2e40cdf28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\pm 1.798" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499790} {:answer/id #uuid "b7ec9ecf-c932-5246-8583-70554e17a596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\pm 2.229" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500028} {:answer/id #uuid "bb64f93f-9e2f-511c-a48a-9bf774a699c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\pm 2.606" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500038} {:answer/id #uuid "df47b00f-4283-5d5f-9724-575f868a3802" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\pm 2.438" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500116} {:answer/id #uuid "fa18b279-af23-595c-b41f-d1624f2de0d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\pm 1.968" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb64f93f-9e2f-511c-a48a-9bf774a699c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\pm 2.606" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500038} :answer-field/id #uuid "78afa67e-d9b8-56b9-b313-ff0bb8f6f118" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "55964901-1ac8-5dce-b560-5abe87c95ae4" :question/math-academy-id "q-159649" :question/problem "![](images/39/39a83b1081d22919c9549c4ff1c6ad8006dc802cc23f9802d762e97883bea48c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=17$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=6$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=23$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=3.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=6$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=12,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=6-12=-6\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{6}^{2}}{17}+\\frac{{3}^{2}}{23}}≈2.606\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -6\\pm 2.606\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500326 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172105 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21ac91dd-ea0e-5432-8431-f5fa4d32c6bb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals Given Two Normal Populations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78efb13f-9673-5a6d-9a43-2da8b02e2061" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\pm 6.395" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499879} {:answer/id #uuid "a46fffa5-c8aa-536d-bf41-d98cefa06600" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\pm 6.589" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499976} {:answer/id #uuid "bb498ffd-75c3-5362-8360-9312a52ca3f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\pm 5.873" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500037} {:answer/id #uuid "c6c0effa-857f-52b0-8b89-9e48f3ef3215" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\pm 5.789" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500056} {:answer/id #uuid "eb5457ab-70ab-5540-acf8-02a050045282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\pm 6.489" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78efb13f-9673-5a6d-9a43-2da8b02e2061" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\pm 6.395" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499879} :answer-field/id #uuid "1eb71659-be46-5935-ba67-718a939af1f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "916f2a38-b006-5a12-8bd4-a4cea4898a65" :question/math-academy-id "q-159650" :question/problem "![](images/64/6486d3914bb0f7b4898220512e96630d13d2b0f4a5c1851938f98371a4c0255f.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=26$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=21$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=8.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=17$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=20,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=17-20=-3\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{9}^{2}}{26}+\\frac{{8}^{2}}{21}}≈6.395\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -3\\pm 6.395\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172105 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21ac91dd-ea0e-5432-8431-f5fa4d32c6bb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals Given Two Normal Populations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60d30b43-7393-5a6d-8a00-faffee94d6df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\pm 7.435" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499843} {:answer/id #uuid "84a1ad66-15d0-56cc-8056-5f7dce60061c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\pm 7.683" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499908} {:answer/id #uuid "a0e4826f-6da2-57e3-8a4c-9428bfbbc0ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\pm 7.221" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499965} {:answer/id #uuid "b3ae43f2-6b8a-5de1-a2b1-e342b57223eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pm 6.846" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500015} {:answer/id #uuid "d6e3437f-42a6-599c-a6f6-285f76c3e2d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pm 6.679" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0e4826f-6da2-57e3-8a4c-9428bfbbc0ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\pm 7.221" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499965} :answer-field/id #uuid "b17dccba-23c2-5ab8-b49f-4b01afd89316" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6ff97d1-caaf-54b3-95a7-b3c5d233fee4" :question/math-academy-id "q-159651" :question/problem "![](images/64/6486d3914bb0f7b4898220512e96630d13d2b0f4a5c1851938f98371a4c0255f.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=25$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=10$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=21$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=9.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=15$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=16,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=15-16=-1\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{10}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{9}^{2}}{21}}≈7.221\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -1\\pm 7.221\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
